        787.0,
        340.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Matrix multiplication is a little more involved. We start by considering the dot product of two vectors, as this forms the basic operation of matrix multiplication. Letting a and b be two n-dimensional vectors (the first a column vector, the second a row vector), their dot product a · b is a scalar given by",
      "source_page": 200,
      "printed_page": 180,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p200:b5"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        134.0,
        354.0,
        1094.0,
        484.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter8_formula019]]",
      "source_page": 200,
      "printed_page": 180,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p200:b6"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        522.0,
        511.0,
        708.0,
        594.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "For example, for the two vectors",
      "source_page": 200,
      "printed_page": 180,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p200:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        138.0,
        622.0,
        531.0,
        654.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter8_formula020]]",
      "source_page": 200,
      "printed_page": 180,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p200:b8"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        390.0,
        684.0,
        841.0,
        805.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "the dot product is $ \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = (1 \\times 4) + (2 \\times 5) + (3 \\times 7) + (4 \\times 9) = 71 $. Note that the dot product is not defined if the vectors have different lengths.",
      "source_page": 200,
      "printed_page": 180,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p200:b9"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        138.0,
        835.0,
        1095.0,
        900.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Now consider the matrix $ L = MN $ produced by multiplying the $ r \\times c $ matrix M by the $ c \\times b $ matrix N. Partitioning M as a column vector of r row vectors,",
      "source_page": 200,
      "printed_page": 180,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p200:b10"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        139.0,
        900.0,
